<mujoco model="ur5e_pendulum_realfriction">
  <!--
  ============================================================
  THE HARDWARE ATTACHMENT, WITH REAL MEASURED HINGE FRICTION.
  Created 2026-08-14. Byte-identical to pendulum_attachment.xml
  EXCEPT the two hinge friction numbers.
  ============================================================
  Geometry, masses, meshes, hinge axis (local Z) and all provenance are
  unchanged from pendulum_attachment.xml -- read that file for the full
  CAD-provenance discussion, which is not duplicated here.

  THE ONLY CHANGE:
      damping      0.02   -> 0.0001  Nms/rad
      frictionloss 0.01   -> 0.001   Nm
  These are the REAL hardware values. The originals are unvalidated CAD
  placeholders with no provenance (flagged as such in
  docs/status/pendulum_balance_friction_sweep_2026-08-10.md), and the error
  is not small -- it is ~200x in viscous damping and it INVERTED a headline
  conclusion. With the placeholders this pendulum has zeta = 3.97, i.e.
  OVERDAMPED and physically unable to oscillate, with a ~21 deg stiction
  dead-band around BOTH equilibria; a swing-up feasibility study correctly
  concluded on that model that kick energy saturates at ~9% of E_top and no
  finite number of kicks converges. With the real values zeta = 0.019 and the
  dead-band is 2.06 deg, and a single guard-clean kick carries ~1.9x the
  energy needed to reach inverted.

  INTENDED PAIRING -- this asset's local-Z hinge only works at wrist_2 ~ 0:
      ARM_Q0 = [-2.3688, -2.1801, -1.8838, -0.7962, 0.004714693, 0.0206]
      -> hinge 89.73 deg from vertical (works), kappa = 0.7165, mgr = 0.027870
    At the well-conditioned OLD_POSE (wrist_2 ~ 89.5 deg) the same hinge is
    11.25 deg from vertical -> mgr = 0.005439, effectively dead. Use
    pendulum_attachment_realrod.xml there instead. Axis and pose are ONE
    decision, not two -- re-measure both before using this at any other pose.

  NOTE ON ARM_Q0: it sits essentially ON the UR5e wrist singularity
  (cond(J) = 1395.76). Measured consequence for cart speed under the
  |qd| < 3.0 rad/s guard: only 0.0096 m/s if end-effector ORIENTATION is
  held, but 1.0183 m/s if orientation is left free -- a 106x difference. The
  singularity costs orientation authority specifically, not X/Z position
  authority. Any fast motion at this pose therefore requires relaxing the
  orientation task.
  -->
  <!--
  Standalone MJCF for the physical "UR5e Pendulum v1" apparatus
  (cartpole_cad/UR5e Pendulum v1.zip) -- the free-swinging "pole" this
  workspace's original "real_Cartpole" name refers to, mounted at the
  UR5e's wrist_3 attachment_site via an OnRobot tool-changer.

  Meant to be composed onto the existing, PROTECTED centerpiece model
  (assets/ur5e_torque/ur5e_torque.xml, see AGENTS.md sec 2 -- "never
  delete or silently regenerate it") via mujoco.MjSpec.attach(site=...)
  at that model's "attachment_site" -- see
  simulation/ur5e_pendulum_compose.py for the loader. This file is NEVER
  included via plain <include> into ur5e_torque.xml or its scene.xml;
  it has no <worldbody> root itself so it cannot be loaded standalone as
  a top-level model (this is intentional -- it is an attachment
  fragment, not a scene).

  ============================================================
  DIMENSION PROVENANCE -- read before trusting any number below
  ============================================================
  The CAD (cartpole_cad/UR5e Pendulum v1.zip) is a genuine SolidWorks
  assembly (UR5e Pendulum v1.SLDASM) with real custom parts (Pendulum
  Rod, Rod Housing, Shaft Housing, Sensor Housing, Linear Motion Rod,
  8mm Bore Clamp) plus catalog hardware (McMaster fasteners, an MF128ZZ
  flanged ball bearing) and a CUI/Same Sky AMT222B-8000-S single-turn
  absolute magnetic encoder assembly. SolidWorks .SLDPRT/.SLDASM files
  are a proprietary binary format this environment has no reader for
  (no SolidWorks, no working translator found -- cadquery/OCP was
  installed and DOES read the two open-format .STEP files in the
  archive, but not the native SolidWorks parts).

  What is REAL (extracted or directly inferred from the archive):
    - OnRobot tool-changer robot-side plate: 71mm x 71mm x 16.1mm
      (bounding box, read via cadquery from
      "ST-RB-028-0004 OnRobot Tool Changer (Robot Side).STEP").
    - Tool-side plate bounding box: 75.51mm x 28.40mm x 71.00mm (same
      method, "...0023...Tool Side.STEP").
    - Rod/shaft diameter = 8mm -- not measured, but two independently
      named parts in the archive reference "8mm" explicitly ("8mm Bore
      Clamp", "Same_Sky_AMT-Sleeve-REV-D(8 mm)"), a real, non-arbitrary
      signal for this one dimension.
    - The encoder is a SINGLE-TURN ABSOLUTE type (CUI/Same Sky AMT22
      series) -- confirmed by part family, exact bit-resolution not
      confirmed (the "-8000-S" suffix was not decoded with confidence,
      not asserted here).

  What is PLACEHOLDER (physically reasonable, NOT measured -- flagged
  explicitly on every such value below, must be corrected against the
  real CAD/hardware before this model is used for anything beyond a
  first smoke test):
    - Pendulum rod LENGTH: CORRECTED 2026-08-11 to 0.12 m, a real
      measured value (was a 0.30 m PLACEHOLDER guess before this date --
      see git history for the prior text). No longer a placeholder.
    - Rod/housing MASS: PARTLY RESOLVED 2026-08-12. The apparatus TOTAL
      (~0.300 kg) and the pole (~0.050 kg) are now real measured values,
      supplied directly, and the model reproduces them -- see the MASS
      BUDGET note on the tool-changer geoms below. What is still inferred
      is (a) the split of the remaining 0.250 kg across the two plates
      and the housing cluster, and (b) the mild-steel density
      (7850 kg/m^3) used to derive the rod's mass from its dimensions,
      since "Linear Motion Rod" + a ball-bearing pivot reads as a
      hardened-steel linear/rotary shaft component. That density
      inference is now independently corroborated for the rod: it
      predicts 0.0474 kg, within 5% of the measured ~0.050 kg pole.
    - Pivot joint AXIS ORIENTATION relative to attachment_site -- as of
      2026-08-12 this is local Z, the mounting-face NORMAL, i.e. the
      physically standard convention for a shaft/bearing housing bolted
      to a flat plate, and it is measured to be operable (horizontal in
      world, real X-pumping coupling) at the arm pose the pendulum
      scripts actually use. It is still NOT derived from the CAD
      assembly's actual mate definitions (which are unreadable), so it
      remains an inference from convention rather than a confirmed fact.
      If the real mechanism's swing plane differs, this needs correcting.
      Full reasoning and measurements: the joint element's own comment.
    - No discrete "bob" end-mass is modeled -- the archive's part list
      has no separate weighted part beyond the rod/housings, so mass is
      modeled as distributed along a uniform rod plus a small lumped
      mass for the encoder/housing cluster near the pivot. If the real
      assembly has a separate end weight, this is wrong and needs a
      dedicated body added.

  Do not treat any PLACEHOLDER value as ground truth for anything beyond
  a first-pass kinematic/dynamic sanity check.
  -->

  <default>
    <default class="pendulum">
      <joint damping="0.0001" frictionloss="0.001"/>
      <geom contype="0" conaffinity="0" density="7850"/>
    </default>
  </default>

