      <!-- MESH FRAME CONVENTION -- re-derived from the raw STL files
           2026-08-11 (triangle-area-weighted face-normal histogram + slab
           profiles, not assumed). MuJoCo recenters/reorients mesh vertices
           to their own inertial frame at compile time (mesh_pos/mesh_quat)
           but COMPOSES the inverse into geom_pos/geom_quat, so a geom pos
           here is interpreted in each STL's OWN native file frame --
           verified by pushing the compiled vertices back through
           geom_xpos/geom_xmat and confirming the resulting bounding boxes
           land exactly where these numbers say.

           robot side: file bbox [-0.0355,-0.0355,-0.0025] .. [0.0355,0.0355,
           0.0136], i.e. a 71.0 x 71.0mm disc 16.1mm thick along file +Z
           (area-weighted flat-face area perpendicular to Z is 138cm^2 vs
           48/59cm^2 for X/Y -- Z really is the plate normal, as the earlier
           comment assumed). Mounted so its extreme -Z face lands exactly on
           attachment_site: pos z = +0.0025, occupying mount z in [0, 0.0161].

           tool side: FIXED 2026-08-11, it was mounted 90deg wrong. File bbox
           [-0.0355, 0.0, -0.0355] .. [0.0400, 0.0284, 0.0355]: the plate
           normal is file +Y (thickness 28.4mm; area-weighted flat-face area
           perpendicular to Y is 183cm^2 vs 117/127cm^2 for X/Z), and the
           file's y=0 plane IS the coupling face (a solid 63mm-dia disc at
           y in [0,0.002], the part growing only in +y). The old code stacked
           it along +Z with no quat, which stood the 71mm plate ON EDGE and
           made the stack 71mm tall instead of 28.4mm. The earlier comment
           had actually spotted this ("its shortest dimension (28.4mm) is Y,
           not Z ... NOT a simple flat plate along Z") and mounted it that way
           regardless. quat here is +90deg about X, mapping file +Y -> mount
           +Z, so the coupling face sits on the robot-side plate's outer face
           at mount z = 0.0161 and the stack ends at z = 0.0445.
           Also dropped the old "-0.00225" X shift: that came from the bbox
           CENTER, but the bbox is asymmetric only because of a mounting lug
           at +x; the Ø71 plate itself is centered on file x=0 (slab profile
           at y in [0.014,0.016] is x in [-0.0355,+0.0355]), so re-centering
           on the bbox was pushing the plate 2.25mm off the tool axis.
           The lug's azimuth about the tool axis is arbitrary here -- not
           evidence-backed, and nothing depends on it.

           MASS BUDGET -- explicit mass= added 2026-08-12, replacing
           density-derived mass, which was badly wrong. The class="pendulum"
           default density=7850 made MuJoCo compute 0.759 kg (robot side) +
           1.307 kg (tool side) = 2.066 kg for the two plates, so the whole
           apparatus weighed 2.17 kg -- 7x the real hardware. Two independent
           checks caught it: (1) the user measured the real assembly at
           ~0.300 kg total with a ~0.050 kg pole; (2) MuJoCo's density-based
           mesh mass is a bounding-box-class approximation, not the true
           volume -- its tool-side figure implies 166 cm^3, LARGER than that
           part's own 152 cm^3 file-frame bounding box, i.e. physically
           impossible. Integrating the STLs properly (divergence theorem over
           the triangle soup) gives true volumes of 21.90 cm^3 (robot side)
           and 50.38 cm^3 (tool side).

           The 0.300 kg total is the real measurement and is what this file
           now reproduces. It is allocated as:
             pendulum_rod         0.0474 kg  density x volume, unchanged --
                                             Ø8mm x 0.12m steel. Independent
                                             cross-check: this falls out of
                                             the archive-supported diameter
                                             and the measured rod length,
                                             and lands within 5% of the
                                             separately-measured ~0.050 kg
                                             pole. Two unrelated routes to
                                             the same number.
             toolchanger_robot_side 0.0606 kg }  0.200 kg split by the two
             toolchanger_tool_side  0.1394 kg }  MEASURED true mesh volumes
                                                 above (30.3% / 69.7%), the
                                                 only evidence available for
                                                 their relative mass.
             pendulum_hub         0.0500 kg  remainder (housing + encoder).
             ------------------------------  total 0.2974 kg.
           Only the TOTAL is measured; the split between plates rests on the
           volume ratio and the hub is the free remainder. If a real per-part
           breakdown is ever measured, correct the split here -- but keep the
           total at the measured value. Do NOT revert to density-derived mass
           for these two meshes: coarsened tessellation plus MuJoCo's
           bounding-box approximation makes that route unusable here. -->
      <!-- NOTE: this 2.17 kg -> 0.30 kg correction is a large change to the
           payload the arm carries. Every gain/equilibrium derived against the
           old model (pendulum_balance_torque_lqr.py, the energy-shaping
           constants, find_inverted_angle) must be re-derived, not assumed. -->
      <geom name="toolchanger_robot_side" type="mesh" mesh="toolchanger_robot_side_mesh"
            pos="0 0 0.0025" mass="0.0606" class="pendulum" rgba="0.35 0.35 0.38 1"/>
      <geom name="toolchanger_tool_side" type="mesh" mesh="toolchanger_tool_side_mesh"
            pos="0 0 0.0161" quat="0.70710678 0.70710678 0 0"
            mass="0.1394" class="pendulum" rgba="0.3 0.3 0.32 1"/>

      <!-- Encoder/sensor housing cluster (Shaft Housing + Sensor Housing
           + MF128ZZ bearing + 8mm Bore Clamp + AMT222 assembly), lumped as
           one small mass at the pivot.
           mass 0.05 kg -- still the least-supported number in the mass
           budget below (no separate measurement exists for the housing
           cluster on its own); it is the remainder after the rod and the
           two tool-changer plates, chosen to close the measured 0.300 kg
           total. See the MASS BUDGET note on the tool-changer geoms above.

           pos z = 0.0445 (CHANGED 2026-08-12): flush against the outer face
           of the tool-changer stack, which ends at exactly that z. It was
           0.0595 while this housing geom was a LATERAL cylinder of radius
           0.020 -- centring it 1.5cm past the stack made the cylinder's
           BARREL just touch the stack's end face, which is what "sits
           immediately under the tool-changer stack" meant then. With the
           geom re-oriented AXIALLY (see below) the same intent is expressed
           by butting its END CAP against the stack, i.e. pos = 0.0445;
           leaving 0.0595 would have opened a fresh 1.5cm visual gap -- the
           exact "floating tool" complaint this assembly has already been
           fixed for once. (Before 2026-08-11 it was 0.071, sized against the
           old on-edge tool-side plate that wrongly made the stack 71mm
           tall.) Moving this origin ALONG the hinge axis changes no pendulum
           dynamics -- see the rod's own note below on why an along-axis
           translation leaves both the gravity torque and the moment of
           inertia about the hinge exactly unchanged.

           SHAPE: a cylinder running ALONG THE HINGE AXIS from the mounting
           face outward -- the physical shaft housing carrying the 8mm shaft,
           the MF128ZZ bearing and the encoder. Introduced 2026-08-11 (then
           along local X, when the hinge was local X); RE-ORIENTED 2026-08-12
           to local Z together with the hinge axis (see the joint element's
           comment for that decision). Its 0.06m length -- the load-bearing
           dimension here -- is unchanged; only the DIRECTION changed, and
           the new direction is the physically standard one: a shaft housing
           bolted to a flat plate stands off along that plate's NORMAL, which
           is local Z. It also still keeps the assembly visually CONTINUOUS
           from flange -> plates -> housing -> rod, which was the point of
           the 2026-08-11 fix.

           The properties that made this shape worth having are preserved
           exactly, because they are stated relative to the hinge and geom
           and hinge were rotated together -- the geom is COAXIAL with the
           hinge, so:
             - it is visually static under rotation (a cylinder spinning
               about its own axis), matching a real fixed housing even though
               it is modeled on the rotating body;
             - its centre of mass is ON the hinge axis, so it contributes
               exactly zero gravity torque -- the pendulum's equilibria are
               set by the rod alone;
             - its inertia about the hinge is 0.5*m*r^2 = 1.0e-5 kg m^2 for a
               coaxial cylinder whichever axis that is, i.e. unchanged by the
               re-orientation and negligible against the rod's ~2.3e-4.

           WRIST CLEARANCE -- the thing this piece exists to buy -- is now
           bought differently, and was RE-MEASURED rather than assumed. Under
           the old local-X hinge the rod's swing disc lay in a plane
           CONTAINING the tool axis, so it swept straight through
           wrist_3_link unless the housing first carried it 0.06m sideways
           (measured then: 1.6cm min clearance with that offset, 4.3cm of
           penetration without it). With the hinge on local Z the swing disc
           is PERPENDICULAR to the tool axis and sits 0.1045m out along it,
           past the end of the entire mounting stack -- it no longer
           intersects the wrist at all, and the clearance no longer depends
           on a lateral offset. Measured on the composed model over the full
           +-180deg hinge sweep at the current ARM_Q0 (mj_geomDistance, all
           29 arm geoms vs the two moving hub/rod geoms): min arm-to-pendulum
           distance 4.45cm, vs 1.6cm before, and it is the STATIC housing-to-
           wrist_2_link gap, i.e. theta-independent -- the swinging rod never
           comes closest to the arm at all now. What the rod does come close
           to at this pose is the FLOOR: min 6.34cm at theta = -3.04 rad
           (tip-site world Z range over the sweep [0.063, 0.303] m). That is
           clearance, not contact, but it is a real and much smaller margin
           than the 0.268m the previous arm pose had -- almost entirely
           because the new ARM_Q0 holds the flange at world z = 0.184m rather
           than 0.328m, not because of this geometry change. Worth re-
           measuring for any new arm pose. Still re-run
           pendulum_balance_torque_lqr.py / find_inverted_angle after any
           geometry change rather than assuming old gains carry over. -->
      <body name="pendulum_hub" pos="0 0 0.0445">
        <geom name="pendulum_hub_geom" type="cylinder" fromto="0 0 0  0 0 0.06" size="0.020" class="pendulum" rgba="0.15 0.15 0.18 1" mass="0.05"/>

        <!-- The single swing DOF -- represents the AMT222 absolute
             encoder's measured axis.

             ============================================================
             2026-08-12 (later, superseding the same date's earlier note):
             axis is now local Z = "0 0 1", the MOUNTING-FACE NORMAL.
             ============================================================
             HISTORY, kept because it is why this attribute churned twice.
             The original value was local Y, rejected 2026-08-11 because at
             the then-current transport pose local Y mapped to world Z
             (vertical) -- a hinge about a vertical axis gets zero gravity
             torque at every angle and cannot function as a pendulum
             (confirmed empirically: the rod stayed frozen under
             X-oscillation forcing). It was replaced with local X, which was
             horizontal at that pose. Local Z was then evaluated at that
             same pose earlier on 2026-08-12 and MEASURED to be unusable
             THERE (peak gravity torque 0.008310 Nm vs this joint's own
             0.01 Nm Coulomb frictionloss -> stiction-locked at every
             angle; a full k_e / kick sweep reached 2.1-2.6 deg of arc vs
             174 deg and a genuine flip for local X). That measurement was
             correct and is not retracted -- but its conclusion ("keep
             local X") was conditional on an arm pose that has since
             changed, and it said so explicitly.

             WHY LOCAL Z IS THE RIGHT AXIS, on physical grounds first.
             A rotary shaft carried in a housing bolted flush to a flat
             mounting plate protrudes along that plate's NORMAL. Local Z is
             this apparatus's mounting-stack direction by construction --
             the two tool-changer plates stack along +Z and the shaft
             housing extends along +Z away from attachment_site -- so the
             standard convention puts the hinge along local Z. Local X and
             local Y both lie IN the plane of the mounting face, which is
             where a shaft is NOT. That argument was already stated (and
             conceded) in the rejected-2026-08-12 note above; what was
             missing was an arm pose at which it is also operable.

             WHY IT IS NOW ALSO OPERABLE -- measured, at the real pose.
             The arm pose used by every pendulum script in tools/diagnostics/
             (pendulum_swingup_energy_shaping.py::ARM_Q0) is now the user's
             actual real-hardware UR5e configuration,
               q = [-2.3688, -2.1801, -1.8838, -0.7962, 0.004714693, 0.0206]
             (wrist_2 wrapped into the model's valid range from a real
             6.2879 rad probe). Measured on the composed model, attachment_
             site's axes land in world there as:
               local X -> [-0.0941, -0.0851,  0.9919]   ( 7.29 deg from
                                                          VERTICAL)
               local Y -> [ 0.7103,  0.6924,  0.1268]
               local Z -> [-0.6976,  0.7164, -0.0047]   (89.73 deg from
                                                          VERTICAL,
                                                          45.76 deg from
                                                          world X)
             A hinge driven by the arm's world-X transport motion must
             satisfy two independent conditions (the same two the earlier
             note derived):
               1. HORIZONTAL in world, or gravity makes no torque about it.
                  local Z is 89.73 deg from vertical here -- essentially
                  perfect (sin = 0.99999).
               2. NOT PARALLEL to world X, or the pivot's X acceleration
                  makes no torque about it either. |Zhat x xhat| = 0.7165
                  here -- real coupling, though weaker than the 1.000 the
                  old pose gave the old axis. That weaker pumping authority
                  is a genuine cost of this pose and is quantified in the
                  swing-up validation, not hidden here.
             The old axis (local X) is 7.29 deg from VERTICAL at this pose,
             i.e. the two axes have swapped roles -- the axis change and the
             pose change are one decision, not two, and neither is valid
             without the other.

             NOTE, as before: orthogonality guarantees nothing. Each
             candidate axis must be measured at the pose actually in use;
             "the other non-vertical axis" is not a substitute for a
             measurement. Re-derive if the pose family changes again.

             SEPARATE CONCERN, deliberately not conflated: this pose sits
             essentially ON the classic UR wrist_2=0 kinematic singularity
             (wrist_2 = 0.0047 rad, cond(J6) = 1396 at the pose itself).
             That is an ARM-side Jacobian-conditioning problem affecting how
             accurately the arm can execute the kicking motion; it has no
             bearing on the pendulum's own gravity dynamics, which depend
             only on the hinge axis's orientation in world. Keep the two
             apart when reasoning about failures here.

             STILL A PLACEHOLDER in one respect: this is an inference from
             standard mounting convention plus a measurement of what works
             at the real pose -- it is NOT CAD-verified against the real
             assembly's actual mate geometry (the SolidWorks mates are
             unreadable in this environment, see the provenance note at the
             top of this file). If the physical apparatus's swing plane is
             ever confirmed to differ, fix it here. -->
        <joint name="pendulum_hinge" type="hinge" axis="0 0 1" pos="0 0 0" class="pendulum" range="-3.14159 3.14159" limited="false"/>
        <site name="pendulum_hinge_site" pos="0 0 0" size="0.004" rgba="1 0 0 1"/>

        <!-- Pendulum rod: 8mm diameter is a real, archive-supported
             value; length corrected 2026-08-11 to the real measured
             value, 0.12m (was a 0.30m PLACEHOLDER guess -- see provenance
             note above, which still applies to the steel density
             assumption). This is a real hardware measurement, supplied
             directly, not another placeholder.

             DIRECTION CHANGED 2026-08-12: the rod now extends along local
             +X, PERPENDICULAR to the hinge axis. It previously extended
             along local +Z (the same direction the mounting stack extends),
             which was correct only while the hinge was local X. With the
             hinge corrected to local Z (see the joint element's comment) a
             +Z rod would be COLLINEAR WITH ITS OWN HINGE AXIS -- an offset
             crank that spins about its own centreline and never visibly
             swings, with exactly zero gravity torque, not a pendulum at all.
             This is a required consequence of the axis fix, not an
             independent modelling choice.
             ONLY the direction changed: length 0.12m, diameter 8mm and the
             density-derived mass are all untouched.
             Local X vs local Y is a free choice here and is NOT physically
             meaningful: the rod sweeps a full circle in the plane
             perpendicular to the hinge, so the choice only sets which raw
             theta value gets labelled "hanging" at a given arm pose (that
             label is read off the model by find_inverted_angle, never
             assumed). Local +X is kept because it is the direction the
             housing used to extend, minimising churn in the surrounding
             geometry.

             IMPORTANT, learned the hard way while fixing the wrist
             collision in 2026-08-10/11: the rod's direction WITHIN its
             swing plane does not by itself buy collision clearance.
             Gravity's "up" is fixed in world space, so the
             "inverted"/balance configuration always requires the rod to
             point the same physical direction at a given arm pose no
             matter which raw theta value is chosen to label it "0" --
             rotating this direction inside the swing plane only relabels
             which number means "hanging" vs "inverted." The same symmetry
             is why the FLOOR constraint cannot be dodged by flipping it:
             the rod points physically downward at one end of the swing
             regardless of labelling. (What DID buy clearance in 2026-08-11
             was the along-axis offset below; what buys it now is that the
             swing plane itself no longer contains the tool axis -- see the
             housing comment above.)
             Mass is computed from density x volume by the compiler (no
             explicit mass override), consistent with the class="pendulum"
             default density above; the re-orientation leaves it unchanged. -->
        <!-- pos z = 0.06 (was pos x = 0.06 until 2026-08-12, re-oriented with
             the hinge axis): the rod is clamped to the OUTBOARD END of the
             shaft housing above, not at the mounting face -- physically, to
             the exposed end of the shaft the housing carries. This is a
             translation ALONG the hinge axis, which leaves both the gravity
             torque about that axis and the rod's moment of inertia about it
             exactly unchanged, so the pendulum's own dynamics and equilibria
             do not depend on this number. It does place the whole swing disc
             0.1045m out along the tool axis (0.0445 hub + 0.06 housing),
             clear of the wrist -- see the measured sweep in the housing
             comment above. -->
        <body name="pendulum_rod" pos="0 0 0.06">
          <geom name="pendulum_rod_geom" type="cylinder" fromto="0 0 0  0.12 0 0" size="0.004" class="pendulum" rgba="0.6 0.6 0.65 1"/>
          <site name="pendulum_tip_site" pos="0.12 0 0" size="0.006" rgba="0 1 0 1"/>
        </body>
      </body>
    </body>
  </worldbody>

  <sensor>
    <!-- Mirrors the real AMT222's role: a single-axis absolute angle
         reading of pendulum_hinge. jointpos on a hinge joint returns
         the joint's own signed angle in radians -- this is the
         simulated equivalent of what the real encoder reports over its
         digital interface (exact resolution/interface not modeled). -->
    <jointpos name="pendulum_angle_sensor" joint="pendulum_hinge"/>
  </sensor>
</mujoco>
